<p>I'm Mike, ACT's tech lead, and I'll be the one running Booth Camp.&nbsp;</p><ul><li><strong>Why this exists:</strong> Right now, a lot of our tech knowledge--the cue-sheet process, our QLab setup, how our lights and sound actually work--lives mostly in my head. That's fine until it isn't. I've trained one other person so far, which is better than nothing, but it's not a deep bench. This course is my attempt to fix that. I can't make everyone an expert overnight, but I can give a small group of you a real, documented foundation to build from.</li>


&nbsp; <li><strong>No experience needed:</strong> Never run lights or sound before? Never opened QLab (the software we use for it)? Completely fine--Session 1 only assumes you're willing to read a script closely, and Session 2 only assumes you've done Session 1. If you've run sound or lights elsewhere, some of this will feel familiar; that's fine too. Everyone leaves with the same manual and the same hands-on experience either way.</li>


&nbsp; <li><strong>What you'll walk away with:</strong> By the end of the two days, you'll have personally built and run an actual cue sheet through an actual QLab show, start to finish, on our theater's real equipment--not a hypothetical setup, not someone else's gear. You'll also keep a printed, tabbed manual that's still useful the next time you're in the booth.</li>


&nbsp; <li><strong>The practical stuff:</strong>&nbsp;The $15 materials fee covers printing. I'm capping this at 6 people, purely because that's what our current computers can support hands-on, not because I don't want more of you there. If interest outpaces that, we'll run it again.</li>


&nbsp; <li><strong>One honest ask:</strong> This is a small, paired group, so your attendance affects everyone else's experience, not just your own. If you sign up, plan on both days--not just the parts that sound interesting.</li>


&nbsp; <li><strong>QLab isn't as scary as it looks:</strong> It's an industry standard for a reason. From what I've seen in online discussion groups, theaters basically fall into three camps--physical boards, QLab, or some other software solution--with some overlap (boards for lights/sound, QLab for multimedia). Among the software camp, QLab leads by a significant margin. If it were <em>that</em> hard to learn, it wouldn't be pulling that kind of weight--especially since the company behind it is, as far as I can tell, one person with no outside investment, running on word of mouth.</li>
</ul>


<p>Thank you, genuinely, for even considering giving up two days for this. Learning something new can feel a little exposed when you're doing it in front of other people. I promise this is a low-pressure room--nobody's grading you. We're just trying to build a stronger, more resilient tech team together.</p>